1
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:03,590
This single stroke is the character for “one.”

2
00:00:04,247 --> 00:00:08,807
Let’s find it in three different character tables.

3
00:00:09,497 --> 00:00:12,567
In the Chinese table, it is in row 50, column 27.

4
00:00:13,247 --> 00:00:15,947
In the Japanese table, it is in row 16, column 76.

5
00:00:16,663 --> 00:00:21,023
In the Korean table, the same character is in row 76, column 73.

6
00:00:21,705 --> 00:00:26,325
Can we give it one number and still recover the original document?

7
00:00:27,247 --> 00:00:32,237
In the 1980s, a team at Xerox was working on a Chinese font.

8
00:00:32,913 --> 00:00:38,793
Lee Collins and his colleagues compared Japanese and Chinese tables to find matching characters.

9
00:00:39,477 --> 00:00:44,707
A match in another table could help them extend their Chinese font.

10
00:00:45,423 --> 00:00:50,413
At Apple, exchanging files between different computers also raised questions about text.

11
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:57,300
In 1988, Joe Becker proposed character codes that different systems could share.

12
00:00:57,997 --> 00:01:02,907
Each system would only need a way to convert between its own table and the shared one.

13
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:07,190
The original proposal used 16 bits per character.

14
00:01:07,872 --> 00:01:13,212
English text took more space, but the start of the next character was easy to find.

15
00:01:14,072 --> 00:01:18,142
Now look at these two forms of the character for blue-green.

16
00:01:18,928 --> 00:01:22,048
Look at the lower halves. The strokes inside differ.

17
00:01:22,705 --> 00:01:26,105
The Taiwanese character table gave the two forms distinct positions.

18
00:01:26,747 --> 00:01:31,697
Give them one number and you lose track of which form the original document contained.

19
00:01:32,372 --> 00:01:36,552
That is why each kept its own Unicode code point.

20
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:42,527
In February 1990, a meeting in Seoul addressed Han unification.

21
00:01:43,205 --> 00:01:48,085
The Korean delegation proposed a group in which different countries could review the characters together.

22
00:01:48,727 --> 00:01:53,207
The group held its first formal meeting in Tokyo the following July.

23
00:01:53,913 --> 00:02:00,263
They would check the combined Chinese and Unicode list against rules developed by members of the Japanese delegation.

24
00:02:01,195 --> 00:02:05,305
In October 1991, the first Unicode volume appeared.

25
00:02:05,997 --> 00:02:10,677
Page 109 says the material is under review and will appear in the second volume.

26
00:02:11,372 --> 00:02:15,792
The teams needed time to coordinate with the international standard and review the Han repertoire together.

27
00:02:16,707 --> 00:02:21,527
Eight months later, the second volume’s cross-reference table brings us back to that single stroke.

28
00:02:22,205 --> 00:02:26,035
The three table entries for “one” share a single Unicode code point.

29
00:02:26,705 --> 00:02:30,605
Convert it back to the Korean table, and it is row 76, column 73 again.

30
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:35,898
The aim was to share a character while preserving the distinctions in the original text.
